.TH MAAK 1 "2026" "maak 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
maak \- run project tasks written in GNU Guile Scheme
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BR maak
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ TASKS ]
.RI [ TASK_ARGS ] ...
.PP
.BR maak
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI \-\- \ TASKS ...
.RI [ \-\- \ TASK_ARGS ] ...
.PP
.BR maak
.B explain
.I TASK
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B maak
is a command runner and control plane: instead of a bespoke build DSL it uses
the full GNU Guile Scheme language, so a task is simply a procedure in a file
called a
.IR "Maak file" .
.PP
By default
.B maak
loads
.B ./maak.scm
and runs the task named
.BR default .
When that file is absent it reads a task file instead, the first of
.BR ./maak.yaml ,
.BR ./maak.yml ,
.B ./maak.toml
or
.BR ./maak.json .
A command line that asks only for the help screen or the version reads no Maak
file at all, so
.B maak \-\-version
answers in any directory, while a run that would execute a task says there is
no Maak file to read.
.B maak explain
.I TASK
reads the file without running anything, and describes what the task calls,
how its environment would be assembled and which environment file would win.
A Maak file declares the module
.B (maak)
and imports the library:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
(define\-module (maak)
  #:declarative? #t
  #:use\-module (maak maak))

(define (hello)
  "Say hello from Maak!"
  (display "Hello from Maak!\\n"))
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every procedure the module makes visible is a runnable task.  When the module
declares
.BR #:export ,
only the exported procedures are tasks; other procedures become private
helpers.
.PP
The tasks to run are named on the command line, and the words that follow
them are passed to them as their arguments.  Several tasks may be run in one
invocation -- as one comma separated word, or one word each after a
.B \-\-
-- and a
.B \-\-
may set the arguments apart from the tasks they belong to.  See
.B TASKS AND ARGUMENTS
below.
.SH TASKS AND ARGUMENTS
A run names the tasks it is to run, and passes the words that follow them on
to them as their arguments:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak hello                 one task
maak quick\-math 23 42      a task, then its arguments
.EE
.RE
.PP
A
.B \-\-
may set the arguments apart from the task, which is how an argument that
begins with a dash is passed on without being read as an option:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak quick\-math \-\- \-23 42
.EE
.RE
.PP
Several tasks are named as one word, separated by commas:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak clean,build,test
.EE
.RE
.PP
or, when a
.B \-\-
comes before any task word, as one word each:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak \-\- clean build test
.EE
.RE
.PP
So a
.B \-\-
before the first task word ends the options and introduces the task list, and
a
.B \-\-
after a task word ends the task list and starts the arguments of the tasks
named before it.  A run that wants to name its tasks one word each and give
them arguments writes both separators:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak \-\- clean build \-\- \-v
.EE
.RE
.PP
Everything after the separator that begins the arguments is passed on as it
stands:
.B maak
reads no options there, and a word that names one of its own tasks is an
argument like any other.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " " FILE
Load
.I FILE
instead of the file
.B maak
would look for.  It may be a Maak file or a task file in JSON, TOML or YAML.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-env\-file " " FILE
Read
.B NAME=VALUE
assignments from
.I FILE
before the Maak file is loaded.  The flag may be repeated, or several files
given at once separated by colons, as in
.BR PATH .
Without it,
.B MAAK_ENV_FILE
is read, and failing that a
.B .env
file beside the Maak file.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-env\-file
Read no environment file at all, whatever the environment or the checkout
says.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-directory " " DIR
Change to
.I DIR
before anything else: the Maak file (
.BR \-f ,
the default
.BR ./maak.scm )
and the
.B .env
file beside it are then looked up inside
.IR DIR ,
and the directory entered is reported.  The flag may be repeated, or several
directories given at once separated by colons, and they are entered in the
order given.  A directory that is not there is an error rather than a quiet
fallback, so a task never runs somewhere it was not asked to.
.TP
.B \-\-banner
Print the greeting banner.  This is the default, and is what turns the banner
back on after
.B MAAK_NO_BANNER
was exported.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-banner
Skip the greeting banner, keeping everything else the run would print.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display a help screen and exit.  The screen is a resource of the launcher, so
no Maak file is read and the screen is available in any directory.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-errors
Carry on after a task fails, and let the run succeed anyway: each failure is
reported where it happened, as a warning.  This is
.B \-\-keep\-going
with a different verdict.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs " " N
Run up to
.I N
tasks at the same time, each in a worker of its own.  A task's standard output
and standard error are captured and printed as one block, in the order the
tasks were named, as soon as the next block in that order is ready.  A task
that fails stops nothing -- every named task has already been started, so this
flag has
.B \-\-keep\-going
for free -- and the run fails at the end with the tasks that did not succeed.
Without this flag the tasks run one after another.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep\-going
Carry on after a task fails instead of ending the run there.  Each failure is
reported where it happened, naming the task that raised it, and the run then
fails with the list of tasks that did not succeed.  Without this flag the first
failure ends the run, which is what a pipeline of tasks usually wants.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List every task in the loaded Maak file, with its documentation, laid out to be
read: a heading naming the file and how many tasks it holds, then one entry a
task, with the name emphasised, the docstring aligned in one column, and what
the task needs to have finished first under it.  Every documentation line starts
in the same column whatever the task is called; a name wider than that column
keeps a line of its own.  Nothing else is printed around a listing \-\- no
banner and no log lines \-\- so its output is the listing and only the listing.
A task that asked to be left out of the listings is left out altogether.
.TP
.BI "\-\-list=" STYLE
Lay the listing out another way.
.I table
is the aligned listing above and the default;
.I long
is the same table with each task's example invocation under it;
.I plain
is the
.IR name: " documentation"
blocks the listing printed before the table;
.I names
is one task name per line and nothing else, which is what a shell loop or a
picker reads; and
.I json
is the versioned description
.B \-\-list \-\-json
prints.  The layout is attached with an equals sign, so a word after
.B \-\-list
is still a task name.
.TP
.BI "\-\-list\-width " N
Fold a listing's docstrings to fit N columns, which is what keeps a long task
description inside an 80\-column terminal.  Without it a docstring is printed as
it was written, so a code sample or an "Args:" table a task laid out stays as it
was laid out.  N must be 40 or more.
.TP
.B \-\-json
With
.BR \-\-list ,
print the listing as JSON instead of as text: one object naming the file, the
root it sits in, and every task with its name, docstring, the line its
definition starts on, the tasks it needs first, an example invocation and the
arguments maak binds, each with its kind, default and the prose the docstring
gives it.  With
.BR \-\-plan ,
print the plan as one versioned JSON object instead.  Nothing else is printed,
so the output is the JSON and only the JSON.  Without
.B \-\-list
or
.B \-\-plan
it is an error, because it describes a listing or a plan rather than asking for
one.
.TP
.B \-\-plan
Run the named tasks with dry\-run semantics and print the plan instead of a
transcript: each command, its arguments, the working directory it would run
in, the environment it was given, and whether it would be elevated or started
in the background.  Nothing is executed.  The task's own Scheme still runs, as
it does in a dry run, so the plan describes what the task decided.  With
.BR \-\-json
the same plan is printed as one versioned object.
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Run the named tasks now, then again whenever a file in the tree changes.  The
Maak file is reloaded for each run, so an edit to the tasks is what the next
run uses; a run that fails is reported and the watch continues.  The environment
files are re-read for every run from the environment the watch started with, and
every run starts in the directory the watch was started in, so a `.env` change
takes effect, a variable a task set does not leak into the run after it, and a
task that changed directory cannot move the run after it.  The tree is
the files git tracks and the untracked files it does not ignore when the
directory is a checkout, and a walk that skips the repository and cache
directories otherwise.  A task's own writes become the next baseline, so a
build directory does not start the task again.  Ctrl\-C stops the watch.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-load\-path " " DIR
Add
.I DIR
to Guile's load path so the Maak file can require modules that live outside
its own directory.  The flag may be repeated, or several directories given at
once separated by colons, as in
.BR PATH .
The directory holding the Maak file, and the directories named by
.BR MAAK_LOAD_PATH ,
are added automatically.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the shell commands a task would run instead of running them.  The same
effect is available inside a task by parameterising
.BR dry\-run? .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-print\-directory
Do not report the directories
.B \-C
changes to.  A run says which directory it entered, because a task that ran
somewhere else is worth knowing about; this is the switch that leaves those
lines out, for a run whose output is read by something other than a person.
.BR \-q
silences them as well, among other things.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the banner, the log lines and the header output.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version, the license and the homepage, then exit.  The version is
the library's rather than the Maak file's, so no Maak file is read: the answer
is the same in a directory that holds none, which is how a script asks which
.B maak
is installed before it decides to run anything.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Let an error reach Guile's own top level handler so the full backtrace is
printed.  Equivalent to setting
.BR MAAK_DEBUG .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tasks " " LIST
Run the tasks named in
.IR LIST ,
a comma separated list.  The tasks named as words on the command line are
turned into this option, which is what
.B maak
hands to the library; naming the tasks here instead leaves every other word on
the line as an argument for them.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GUILE_LOAD_PATH
Guile's own load path; honoured as usual and extended by
.BR \-L .
.TP
.B MAAK_LOAD_PATH
A colon separated list of extra directories to add to the load path.  Unlike
.BR GUILE_LOAD_PATH
it does not replace anything, which makes it convenient to set per project.
.TP
.B MAAK_ENV_FILE
A colon separated list of environment files to read when
.B \-\-env\-file
is not given.  A name that is already in the environment is left alone.
.TP
.B MAAK_NO_BANNER
When set to a non\-empty value, skip the greeting banner.
.B \-\-banner
overrides it.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to a non\-empty value, print no ANSI styling at all: a listing's task
names and a dry run's command lines arrive plain, which is what a file, a log
or a CI job wants.  It is the convention described at
https://no\-color.org, honoured for the whole run.
.TP
.B MAAK_DEBUG
When set to a non\-empty value, print the Guile backtrace on an error instead
of the short maak message.
.TP
.B MAAK_WATCH_INTERVAL
How often
.B \-\-watch
looks at the tree, in seconds.  One second by default; a value that is not a
positive number is an error.
.TP
.B MAAK_CC
The C compiler
.B (maak cc)
uses, falling back to
.B CC
and then
.BR cc .
The value may carry flags, as in
.BR "ccache gcc" .
.TP
.B MAAK_CXX
The C++ compiler, falling back to
.B CXX
and then
.BR c++ .
.TP
.B MAAK_AR
The archiver, falling back to
.B AR
and then
.BR ar .
.TP
.B MAAK_PKG_CONFIG
The pkg\-config program the
.B (maak cc)
queries run; by default
.BR pkg-config .
.TP
.B MAAK_CARGO
The cargo program the
.B (maak cargo)
helpers run, falling back to
.B CARGO
and then
.BR cargo .
The value may carry flags or a toolchain, as in
.BR "cargo +nightly" .
.TP
.B MAAK_SIFT
The sift program the
.B (maak sift)
helpers run, falling back to
.B SIFT
and then
.BR sift .
A path may replace the program, which is how a project lints itself with the
sift in its own checkout.
.TP
.B MAAK_RUSTUP
The rustup program, falling back to
.B RUSTUP
and then
.BR rustup .
.TP
.B MAAK_MAVEN
The Maven program the
.B (maak maven)
helpers run, falling back to
.B MAVEN
and then
.BR mvn .
.TP
.B MAAK_GRADLE
The Gradle program the
.B (maak gradle)
helpers run, falling back to
.B GRADLE
and then
.BR gradle .
.TP
.B MAAK_EMACS
The Emacs program the
.B (maak elisp)
helpers run, falling back to
.B EMACS
and then
.BR emacs .
.TP
.B MAAK_UV
The uv program the
.B (maak python)
helpers run, falling back to
.B UV
and then
.BR uv .
.TP
.B MAAK_UVX
The uvx program, falling back to
.B UVX
and then
.BR uvx .
.TP
.B MAAK_PYTHON
The Python interpreter, falling back to
.B PYTHON
and then
.BR python3 .
.TP
.B MAAK_PACKAGE_MANAGER
The package manager the
.B (maak frontend)
helpers run: one of
.BR npm ,
.BR pnpm
or
.BR yarn ,
or a command whose last word is one of them, as in
.BR "corepack pnpm" .
Without it the lock file decides, and a directory with none of them means
.BR npm .
.SH TASK AUTHORING
A task is an ordinary procedure.  Its docstring is shown by
.B \-\-list
and before the task runs.
.PP
The following helpers are available from
.BR "(maak maak)" ;
all of them honour dry\-run and quiet modes.
.SS Shell
.TP
.B (run argv)
Run a program from a list of strings: the program first, then one string per
argument.  No shell is involved, so a path containing a space stays one
argument.  With
.BR #:verbose? " #t"
the command is logged first.  It accepts
.BR #:env ,
.BR #:sudo? ,
.B #:dev\-null?
and
.B #:allow\-failure?
with the same meanings as
.BR $ .
.TP
.B (run argv #:async? #t)
Start the program in the background and return a job record instead of a
boolean.  Unlike a trailing
.BR & ,
the job has a pid and a status:
.BR job? ,
.BR job\-pid ,
.BR job\-command ,
.BR job\-done? ,
.B job\-exit\-code
and
.BR wait\-job ,
which returns
.B #t
when the exit code is 0.
.PP
With
.BR #:expand? " #t"
the arguments are joined with spaces and handed to the shell, so wildcards,
.B $VAR
and a leading tilde are expanded the way a shell expands them.  This is opt-in:
the shell then sees words rather than arguments.
.TP
.B (run\-pipeline stages)
Run programs one after another with each standard output feeding the next
standard input, without a shell.  Each stage is an argument list shaped like
.BR run 's ,
or a
.B stage
form, which adds the options
.B run
takes for that stage alone:
.BR #:env ,
.B #:sudo?
and
.BR #:tee .
The stage's environment is added to the pipeline's own, later names winning,
and the command line is built the way
.B run
builds one, so an assignment reaches the program in a pipeline where no shell
reads it.
.BR #:env
on the pipeline sets names for every stage of it, and
.BR #:async? " #t"
starts the whole pipeline in the background and returns a job whose exit code
is 0 only when every stage succeeded; a stage is not a job of its own, because
it cannot finish before the stage after it has read what it wrote.
.PP
.B #:tee FILE
keeps a copy of the stage's standard output in FILE while the stage after it
reads the
same bytes, so a task can look at what a middle stage produced; a
.B #:tee
on the last stage prints as well as keeps, as
.B tee
itself does.
.B #:capture? #t
records every stage that way, in temporary files of its own, and returns a
result record instead of printing: one string per stage from
.BR pipeline\-outputs ,
.B pipeline\-ok?
for whether every stage succeeded, and the failures from
.BR pipeline\-errors .
A captured pipeline prints nothing itself, and
.B #:capture? #t
is refused together with
.BR #:async? " #t" .
.PP
Unlike a shell's
.BR | ,
which reports only the last stage, a pipeline reports every stage that failed;
.B #:allow\-failure? #t
returns
.B #f
instead, or the result when the output was captured.
.TP
.B ($ parts)
Run a shell command built from a list of strings, joined by spaces.  This is
the escape hatch, for pipes, redirections, globs,
.B $(...)
and
.B VAR=value
prefixes.
.PP
The command can be decorated with keyword arguments, applied in the order a
shell would write them:
.BR "[sudo] [env A=1] COMMAND [>/dev/null 2>&1]" .
.BR #:env " '((\(dqCI\(dq . \(dq1\(dq))"
sets names for that command alone, given as an alist, as a list of
.B KEY=VALUE
strings, or as one such string.
.BR #:sudo? " #t"
runs the command as root, and
.BR #:async? " #t"
starts it in the background and returns a job.
.BR #:dev\-null? " #t"
discards its output.
.BR #:allow\-failure? " #t"
returns
.B #f
instead of raising when the command exits non\-zero, so the task is told the
command failed rather than having the failure hidden from it.
.PP
The file helpers
.RB ( mkdir\-p ,
.BR cp ,
.BR mv ,
.BR cat ,
.BR touch ,
.B ln\-s ,
.B chmod\-file
and
.BR delete\-file\-recursively )
accept
.BR #:expand? " #t"
too, which is what lets
.B (delete\-file\-recursively "build/*.o" #:expand? #t)
name the files to remove.  A backgrounded
command reports a job rather than having succeeded: wait for it with
.BR wait\-job .
.BR #:async? " also"
works for
.BR syscall .
.TP
.B (syscall command)
Run
.I command
and raise an error when it exits non\-zero.
.TP
.B (capture command)
Run
.I command
and return its standard output as a string.  A command that exits non\-zero
raises an error naming its exit code rather than returning the empty or partial
output it produced; with
.BR #:allow\-failure? " #t"
its output is returned anyway.
.TP
.B (which command)
Return the absolute path of
.IR command ,
or
.BR #f .
.TP
.B (require\-command command)
Like
.BR which ,
but raise a maak error when the command is missing.
.TP
.B (shell\-quote string)
Quote
.I string
so a shell reads it as one literal word.
.SS C and C++
The helpers in
.B (maak cc)
build compiler, linker and archiver commands, and are re\-exported by
.BR "(maak maak)" .
None of them decides that a task does not need to run.
.TP
.B (compile source #:output FILE #:includes DIRS #:defines DEFS #:flags FLAGS #:language LANG #:compiler PROGRAM #:standard STD #:pic? BOOL #:depfile? BOOL)
Compile one
.I source
to an object.  The object defaults to the source with its extension replaced
by
.BR .o .
.B #:includes
and
.B #:defines
become
.B \-I
and
.B \-D
arguments;
.B #:flags
is inserted verbatim;
.B #:standard
becomes
.BR \-std=STD ;
.B #:pic?
adds
.BR \-fPIC ;
and
.B #:depfile?
adds
.BR "\-MMD \-MP" .
.B #:language
is
.B c
or
.BR c++ ,
and
.B #:compiler
overrides the compiler for this command.
.TP
.B (compile-objects sources #:directory DIR #:jobs N #:incremental? BOOL ...)
Compile every source to its object under
.IR DIR ,
with the same keyword arguments as
.BR compile .
With
.BR #:jobs " N"
above one, N compilers run at once.  With
.BR #:incremental? " #t"
an object newer than its source is left alone; without it every object is
compiled.
.TP
.B (link objects #:output FILE #:libraries NAMES #:library-paths DIRS #:flags FLAGS #:link-flags FLAGS #:language LANG #:compiler PROGRAM #:shared? BOOL #:static? BOOL #:soname NAME)
Link
.I objects
into
.IR FILE .
.B #:libraries
and
.B #:library-paths
build
.B \-l
and
.B \-L
arguments.
.B #:flags
is inserted before the objects, where
.B LDFLAGS
goes;
.B #:link-flags
after them, where
.B LDLIBS
and pkg\-config's libs go.
.B #:shared?
adds
.BR \-shared ,
.B #:static?
adds
.BR \-static ,
and
.B #:soname
adds
.BR \-Wl,\-soname,NAME .
.TP
.B (static-library output objects #:archiver PROGRAM)
Write
.I objects
into a static library with
.BR "ar rcs" .
.TP
.B (object-file source #:optional DIRECTORY)
Return the object path for
.IR source ,
in its own directory or in
.IR DIRECTORY .
.TP
.B (up-to-date? output inputs)
Return
.B #t
when
.I output
exists and is not older than every one of
.IR inputs .
A missing output or input is not up to date.  This is the explicit check a task
uses to skip a compile; maak never applies it on its own.
.TP
.B (environment-flags "CFLAGS")
Return the whitespace separated words of the named environment variable.  The
task says which of
.BR CFLAGS ,
.BR CXXFLAGS ,
.BR CPPFLAGS ,
.B LDFLAGS
and
.B LDLIBS
reaches which command; none is read automatically.
.TP
.B (pkg-config-cflags packages)
.B (pkg-config-libs packages #:static? BOOL)
.B (pkg-config-exists? packages)
.B (pkg-config-version package)
The four pkg\-config queries, with cflags and libs returned as lists.  They
run even in a dry run, because pkg\-config reads the system rather than
changing it, and a plan that left out the flags a compile would use would be
wrong.  The program is the
.B pkg-config-command
parameter or
.BR MAAK_PKG_CONFIG .
.TP
.B (install-file source destination #:mode "644")
Install one file, creating its parent directories, with the mode a Makefile's
.B install
target would set.  A
.B DESTDIR
staging prefix is composed by the task.
.TP
.B (install-directory directory #:mode "755")
Create a directory and its parents with the mode.
.SS Reporting a failure
.TP
.B (task\-error message)
Stop the task with
.I message
treated as text with
.B format
directives.  The arguments for those directives are passed with
.BR #:args ,
and what the user should do about it with
.BR #:hint :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
(task-error "no artefact for ~a" #:args (list "linux-x86_64")
            #:hint "Run `maak build' first.")
.EE
.RE
.PP
The message and the hint are printed on stderr without a backtrace, and the
exit status is 1, so a failure reads like one of maak's own.  With
.B \-\-debug
the backtrace is printed as well.
.TP
.B (raise\-maak\-error message #:hint HINT)
The same mechanism for a message that is already formatted.
.TP
.B (maak\-error? exception)
Recognise either of the above once it has been caught.
.SS Declaring what a task is
.TP
.B (task\-metadata! task #:depends DEPENDS #:hidden? HIDDEN #:example EXAMPLE)
Record what a task declares about itself, after its definition:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
(define (push-docker-latest)
  "Push the latest image to Dockerhub."
  (container (list "push" "localhost/maak:latest")))

(task-metadata! push-docker-latest
                #:depends \(aq(package-docker)
                #:example "maak push-docker-latest")
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B #:depends
is a quoted list of task names that must have finished before this one starts.
A dependency says when a task may run, never whether it does: nothing is
remembered between runs, so a task named on the command line runs whether or
not what it needs has changed.  A sequential run and a
.B \-j
run both honour it, and a dependency that names no task, or a cycle, is an
error before anything runs.
.B #:hidden?
keeps the task out of the listings, without making it unrunnable.
.B #:example
is one invocation to show a reader.
.SS Files
.BR cp ", " mv ", " mkdir\-p ", " remkdir\-p ", " delete\-file\-recursively ", " cat ", "
.BR touch ", " ln\-s ", " chmod\-file ", " read\-file ", " write\-file
.SS Directory and environment
.BR with\-directory ", " cd ", " env\-get ", " env\-set! ", " env\-unset! ", " with\-env ", "
.BR with\-env\-vars ", " home\-dir ", " tmp\-dir ", " load\-env\-file ", "
.BR env\-file\-entries
.SS Load paths
.BR add\-load\-path! ", " add\-load\-paths! ", " with\-load\-path ", " split\-load\-path ", "
.BR environment\-load\-paths ", " maak\-load\-path
.SS Pretty multiline strings
.BR string\-lines ", " unlines ", " unwords ", " dedent\-string ", " indent\-string ", "
.BR text
.SS Guix
.BR guix\-shell ", " guix\-container ", " guix\-pack ", " manifest\-shell ", " program\-shell ", "
.BR time\-machine ", " time\-machine\-manifest\-shell ", " time\-machine\-program\-shell
.PP
The first group builds command lines.  The next group asks Guix for values
instead:
.BR guix\-available? ", " guix\-channels ", " guix\-derivation ", " guix\-build ", "
.BR guix\-program ", " guix\-derivation\-at ", " guix\-build\-at ", " guix\-program\-at .
They read the pins
.B time\-machine
would use -- from
.I channels.scm
or from a revision given to the
.B \-at
forms -- and answer with an alist, a store path or a running program, so a task
can decide on what a pin contains.  Nothing in maak imports a
.B (guix ...)
module: the asking happens in
.BR "guix repl" ,
so maak builds and runs on a machine with no Guix, and only a call to one of
these helpers reports that it is missing, with a hint.  The file a query is
written to is removed after it runs, unless Guix refuses to answer: it is then
kept, and the error names it so it can be run by hand.
.SS Bundles
.TP
.B (bundle #:name NAME #:maak-file FILE ...)
Build a self\-contained artefact that runs
.I FILE
on a machine with neither Guile nor maak installed, and return its path.  The
artefact is a
.B guix pack
of a package that installs the Maak file and a launcher and propagates maak,
Guile and the packages named with
.BR #:packages .
.B #:format
chooses
.BR tarball ,
.B squashfs
or
.BR docker ;
a tarball is made relocatable, so it can be extracted anywhere.
.BR #:output
names the file.
.B #:maak-package
is a Scheme expression for the maak package, which is how a project points at
its own build of maak.  With
.BR #:load? " #t"
a docker pack is loaded into the container engine afterwards.
.PP
The generated package is kept when the build fails, and the error names it so
.B guix pack
can be run by hand.  Only the files named are shipped: the Maak file, and the
packages a task's commands need.
.SS Nix
.BR nix\-shell ", " nix\-develop ", " nix\-build ", " nix\-flake\-build ", " nix\-flake\-check ", "
.BR nix\-run
